<p><strong>14-12</strong></p>

<p>(a) We can use potassium permanganate solution to distinguish between 2-propanol and 2-methyl-2-propanol. In acidic condition, KMnO4 oxidizes 2-propanol into acetone which forms the MnO2 brown precipitate and vanishes KMnO4 purple. As tertiary alcohol cannot be oxidized, 2-methyl-2-propanol remains purple.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img height="165" width="569" class="internal" alt="l1IsQiJOeAQwveNQx2hfZ9w-xYRHYVJRv3yEnT-mEZuX4jKjG948NhxXOHKv_l1aYE-yYFU08voqAWI3LDGY9fnM-nTHTbfqh7agEqyG68A76TfjM-yRIAsb3vidv-Hy-ceqqFtLek1uAnaqyg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/269192/l1IsQiJOeAQwveNQx2hfZ9w-xYRHYVJRv3yEnT-mEZuX4jKjG948NhxXOHKv_l1aYE-yYFU08voqAWI3LDGY9fnM-nTHTbfqh7agEqyG68A76TfjM-yRIAsb3vidv-Hy-ceqqFtLek1uAnaqyg?revision=1" /></p>

<p>(b) 1-propanol and 2-propanol first need to be oxidized into propanal and acetone respectively. <img height="154" width="259" class="internal" alt="SwmQM12rsIq2h0KhXmfiUls4VYzbkcnddvCqwpmXeKT-7q1gbKSJd-BjitdF1AQp70chejTp8lTHoEhKeavmvMXpIY57yAPBIA_GMgGUzwlNKGfi4V8b4xivMooXwM-OmOnB44fLhGd-06QbEg" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/269194/SwmQM12rsIq2h0KhXmfiUls4VYzbkcnddvCqwpmXeKT-7q1gbKSJd-BjitdF1AQp70chejTp8lTHoEhKeavmvMXpIY57yAPBIA_GMgGUzwlNKGfi4V8b4xivMooXwM-OmOnB44fLhGd-06QbEg?revision=1" /></p>

<p>Note: we use pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC) in methylene chloride CH2Cl2 to produce aldehyde without further oxidation.</p>

<p>Fehling&rsquo;s test then can be used to determine the presence of an aldehyde. Propanal reacts with Fehling&rsquo;s reagent (Cu2+ in basic solution), forming a brick-red precipitate Cu2O, while acetone cannot react to Fehling&rsquo;s solution, remaining a deep transparent blue color. </p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img height="163" width="509" class="internal" alt="SH_xQ4OyaypUjd98VebsVjqeeBTr7ykwXeENFW0jUPapbBkM8WHblYowmty2YBXNf436_f_6AD17lJKhswNQrbYNmGQoOJxEhq1PUhXnkxjw50xZTUbO2tznfufdxsKf78Os26fCysqqNVfywQ" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/269201/SH_xQ4OyaypUjd98VebsVjqeeBTr7ykwXeENFW0jUPapbBkM8WHblYowmty2YBXNf436_f_6AD17lJKhswNQrbYNmGQoOJxEhq1PUhXnkxjw50xZTUbO2tznfufdxsKf78Os26fCysqqNVfywQ?revision=1" /></p>

<p>(c) We can use Bromine test to distinguished between cyclopentanol and cyclopentene. Bromine reacts rapidly with cyclopentene, in which the reddish brown color disappears quickly without forming HBr gas bubble. Cyclopentanol does not react with bromine. </p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img height="168" width="350" class="internal" alt="l8nRkR2yqeIaJIN-rmz1MM-fWwEHpQucRK6Gg-q-fT5rnITU9_FTx99RMbjntgRIURDHM_ee9FasoMWtB6Tf90zDqDpu3SFkptJTZN9RUsKpCLckOW5BnRoq53dAwVl0pVxWmiXQ2xJaiBceTw" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/269199/l8nRkR2yqeIaJIN-rmz1MM-fWwEHpQucRK6Gg-q-fT5rnITU9_FTx99RMbjntgRIURDHM_ee9FasoMWtB6Tf90zDqDpu3SFkptJTZN9RUsKpCLckOW5BnRoq53dAwVl0pVxWmiXQ2xJaiBceTw?revision=1" /></p>

<p>(d) Besides KMnO4, K2Cr2O7 in acidic condition is another oxidizing agent that can be used to distinguish between cyclopentanol and cyclopentanone. Acidified K2Cr2O7 oxidizes cyclopentanol into cyclopentanone. Evidence for the reaction is the orange solution (Cr2O72-) turns green solution (Cr3+).<br />
1-cyclopentylethanone cannot be oxidized, remaining the orange solution. </p>

