<p><strong>14-20 </strong></p>

<p>Williamson ether synthesis is an SN2 reaction, which favors strong nucleophile and a primary substrate for back-side attack. Since a tertiary alcohol is given, the resulting alkyl halide is also tertiary, which is sterically hindered for SN2 reaction to occur. <img height="43" width="184" class="internal" alt="KvwhH_cMQo9T3xIdmiNhAMP6tcByJpbU9TbGfbPi13INWy_l8cc0Yjya-rE3V4nbpAcpG7_vdpeq18TW_HnkUInOcnn7V1LAB6Kic-zxdu3dFz9yi63VNFgAONmjJJe69YOdCefjmMRqihCzLA" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/269228/KvwhH_cMQo9T3xIdmiNhAMP6tcByJpbU9TbGfbPi13INWy_l8cc0Yjya-rE3V4nbpAcpG7_vdpeq18TW_HnkUInOcnn7V1LAB6Kic-zxdu3dFz9yi63VNFgAONmjJJe69YOdCefjmMRqihCzLA?revision=1" /></p>

<p>The alkoxide then would function as a base, and an elimination reaction would happen instead of SN2 reaction. </p>

<p>An alternative synthesis that is more likely to occur involving the reaction between a tertiary alkoxide and a primary alkyl halide: <img height="44" width="210" class="internal" alt="x2sE2yDBW58taTHZThHyiVKV5xEisUsQJnFet6jkL4tTaygwMGR984Ldcp4AQEf15C0MmSJLlfHuZjQNsYAkX-hOlm01xmPGmPloYnZQ6bUYeKS_vzOUZMtPu7aECXBWGLqt6DEF6ZJ3MKgbVA" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/269230/x2sE2yDBW58taTHZThHyiVKV5xEisUsQJnFet6jkL4tTaygwMGR984Ldcp4AQEf15C0MmSJLlfHuZjQNsYAkX-hOlm01xmPGmPloYnZQ6bUYeKS_vzOUZMtPu7aECXBWGLqt6DEF6ZJ3MKgbVA?revision=1" /></p>



